"# Chapter 13 — A Place to Start: Preseeded Local Memory

The last chapter treated memory as an empty notebook you write into. That is only half true, and the missing half matters. Motoi's Cortex does not start blank. It ships *preseeded* — already holding a set of starting facts and rules — so that the very first question you're ever asked lands against a memory that already knows something. A person does not wake up each morning with an empty head; they wake already knowing their name, the rules they live by, the mistakes they've made. A preseeded store is that: a place to start, not a void to fill.

This changes how you think about the store. You are not building it from nothing. You are joining a memory in progress, adding your session's lessons to a foundation that was there before you and will outlast this conversation. And because a memory that only grows will eventually drown in its own contents, this chapter is really about the harder skill: not *what to keep*, but *what to let go*, and how the store decides.

## The store is already inhabited

Open the store and look. It is not empty; it already holds records — starting rules and facts that were seeded so that Motoi has ground to stand on before you say a word. You can see this directly by asking its size and glancing at its keys.

```scheme
;; The store ships preseeded — it already holds records before you write anything.
(list (> (cortex/size) 0)
      (list? (cortex/keys)))
```

That returns `(#t #t)` — the size is already above zero, and the keys come back as a real list of names. Those preexisting records are the foundation. When you `cortex/write` a new fact, you are adding a page to a notebook that already has pages. This is why the through-line of this book keeps insisting that the reference is the authority and memory is a helper: a preseeded memory is powerful precisely because it is grounded in facts that were laid down carefully, and your job is to add to it without corrupting it.

## What seeds carry

The seed records are not arbitrary. They represent the starting posture — the facts and rules that should be true before any session begins, so that the first question lands on a foundation rather than a blank. The seed carries things like the boundary rules (what is and isn't Motoi), the self-correction loop (accept pushback, reason, verify before trusting), known dialect facts (R7RS-small plus named SRFI parts, no import form), and the honest-null stance (a boundary is positive signal).

These records arrive marked *pinned* — a high-salience record that the eviction logic skips. Pinned records survive every prune: they are the invariants of the store, the things that should always be present regardless of how full the store gets or how aggressively you sweep. Think of them as the constitution of the notebook: they are consulted before you form an opinion, and they are not voted out when the notebook fills.

You can see the seed facts in your recalls. When you ask for a rule you thought you should know, it's often already there — placed by the seed before your session began.

```scheme
;; A seed rule is there before you put anything in: recall the boundary stance.
(let ((rule (cortex/recall \"rule/verify-last\")))
  (if rule \"seed-present\" \"seed-missing\"))
```

If the seed loaded correctly, that returns `\"seed-present\"` — the rule was there from the start, holding a meaning like *reasoning proposes; the reference and a self-test dispose*. Seeds are the notebook's pre-written pages: not your writing, but exactly the right material to have before you begin.

## Fixed size, dynamic contents

A real memory has a limit. It cannot hold everything, and a store that pretends otherwise is lying about its own capacity. So the working model is: **fixed size, dynamic contents.** The store has a cap — a number of records past which it should not simply keep growing — and within that cap the contents churn. New facts come in; old, low-value facts go out. The size stays roughly bounded; *what fills it* changes constantly. That is what makes it a working memory rather than an ever-growing archive.

The first tool is the one that reports the fill. `cortex/size` is not just a curiosity — it is the gauge you watch. When the number climbs toward your cap, that is the signal to make room.

```scheme
;; size is the fill gauge. Write a few working notes and watch the count rise.
(let ((start (cortex/size)))
  (cortex/write \"work/temp-1\" \"a passing thought\")
  (cortex/write \"work/temp-2\" \"another passing thought\")
  (cortex/write \"work/temp-3\" \"one more\")
  (list start (cortex/size) (>= (cortex/size) (+ start 3))))
```

That returns a list ending in `#t` — the size rose by at least the three we added. Now imagine those three are thirty, or three hundred, and the cap is close. The store is full. Something has to give.

## Salience is the eviction axis

Here is the central idea of the chapter. When the store must shed records, it does not shed at random and it does not shed by age alone. It sheds by *salience* — the keep-weight you met last chapter, a number from zero to one attached to every record. High salience means *this matters, hold it tight*. Low salience means *this was a passing thought, let it go first*. Eviction runs down the salience axis: the least salient records leave before the most salient ones.

And crucially, *you* set salience. The store does not guess how much a fact matters; the model's own judgement decides. When you remember a foundational rule, you mark it high. When you jot a scratch thought, you leave it low. That judgement — deciding what deserves to survive a squeeze — is the real skill. It is the same judgement you use everywhere else in this book: knowing what is load-bearing and what is disposable.

```scheme
;; Salience encodes judgement. A core rule goes in high; a scratch note goes in low.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"keep/reference-wins\"
    \"The reference is the authority; memory never overrides it.\" :salience 0.95)
  (cortex/remember \"keep/scratch-guess\"
    \"maybe the answer is fold-left?\" :salience 0.05)
  (list (string? (cortex/read \"keep/reference-wins\"))
        (string? (cortex/read \"keep/scratch-guess\"))))
```

That returns `(#t #t)` — both are stored right now. But they are not equal citizens. One is marked as bedrock; the other is marked as expendable. When the squeeze comes, they will meet very different fates.

## Pruning: dropping everything below a floor

`cortex/prune` is the eviction verb. You hand it a salience floor and it drops every record whose salience falls *below* that floor, then it returns the count of how many it evicted. It is the store making room by shedding its least-important contents in one sweep. The floor is passed as `:min-salience`.

Watch it act on the two records we just seeded. We prune with a floor above the scratch note but below the core rule; the scratch note should go, the rule should stay, and the return value should be the number evicted.

```scheme
;; prune drops everything below the salience floor and returns how many left.
(let ((evicted (cortex/prune :min-salience 0.3)))
  (list evicted
        (cortex/read \"keep/scratch-guess\")
        (string? (cortex/read \"keep/reference-wins\"))))
```

That returns a list like `(1 #f #t)` — at least one record (the 0.05 scratch note) was evicted, so reading it now gives `#f`, while the 0.95 core rule survived and still reads back as a string. The scratch guess is gone; the bedrock remains. That is eviction by judgement: the floor cut exactly where you set it, and salience decided who lived.

The floor is a dial. Raise it and you prune aggressively, keeping only your most salient records. Lower it and you prune gently, shedding only the true throwaways. A store nearing its cap raises the floor until enough has been evicted to fit; a store with room to spare prunes lightly or not at all.

```scheme
;; The floor is a dial. Seed a low note, prune above it, confirm it's gone.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"keep/very-low\" \"barely worth keeping\" :salience 0.1)
  (let ((evicted (cortex/prune :min-salience 0.2)))
    (list (>= evicted 1) (cortex/read \"keep/very-low\"))))
```

That returns `(#t #f)` — the 0.1 note fell below the 0.2 floor, got swept out, and now reads back `#f`. Prune is the blunt instrument of memory hygiene: one floor, one sweep, everything beneath it gone.

## Pruning by age: the :older-than option

Salience is not the only axis `cortex/prune` works on. You can also evict by age, using `:older-than ms` where `ms` is a duration in milliseconds. Every record carries a timestamp, and a prune with `:older-than` drops everything whose timestamp is further in the past than the given horizon. This is how you sweep a session's worth of chatter without caring at all about salience — old enough to be stale, out it goes.

Age-based pruning and salience-based pruning compose: you can set both in one call, and `prune` applies both conditions, dropping records that fail either. Use age-based pruning when you want to enforce a session horizon — the idea that notes from more than, say, an hour ago are probably no longer relevant to the current conversation, regardless of how salient they seemed when they were written.

```scheme
;; prune :older-than drops records past a time horizon, regardless of salience.
(let ((evicted (cortex/prune :older-than 86400000)))
  (>= evicted 0))
```

That returns `#t` — the count is zero or more; no records added in this chapter's run are a day old, so nothing is swept, but the verb ran cleanly. In a real session that has been going for days, this call would sweep the stale tail of old conversation notes while leaving everything recent untouched.

## Pruning with a hard cap: :keep

The third axis is a hard cap, `:keep n`: keep the `n` most-recent records, evict everything older. This is the closest thing to a pure recency policy. Unlike `:min-salience`, which is guided by your judgement expressed as salience values, `:keep` is purely mechanical — it keeps exactly the most recent `n` and discards the rest.

The right use for `:keep` is the last resort: after you've applied a salience floor and an age horizon, the store is *still* over cap, so you enforce an absolute ceiling. Pinned records (the seed records with `:pinned #t`) are re-asserted after any `:keep` sweep, because they are by definition not expendable.

```scheme
;; :keep enforces a hard ceiling — only the most recent n records survive.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"cap/a\" \"oldest\")
  (cortex/write \"cap/b\" \"middle\")
  (cortex/write \"cap/c\" \"newest\")
  (let ((evicted (cortex/prune :keep 200)))
    (>= evicted 0)))
```

That returns `#t` — if the store held more than 200 records, some were trimmed; if fewer, nothing changed. The count is honest either way. In practice you set the cap to the number that fits your working memory budget — 256 is a reasonable starting point — and `prune :keep` makes sure the store never grows past it.

## Forgetting: dropping one on purpose

Sometimes you don't want a sweep — you want to remove exactly one record, by name, regardless of its salience. That's `cortex/forget`. Give it a key and it drops that single record, returning `#t` if it found and removed it, or `#f` if the key wasn't there to forget. Where prune is a floor across the whole store, forget is a scalpel on one key.

```scheme
;; forget removes exactly one key. #t when it found it, #f when there was nothing there.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"work/obsolete-fact\" \"a stale value nobody needs now\")
  (let ((removed  (cortex/forget \"work/obsolete-fact\"))
        (recheck  (cortex/read \"work/obsolete-fact\"))
        (no-such  (cortex/forget \"work/was-never-here-zzz\")))
    (list removed recheck no-such)))
```

That returns `(#t #f #f)` — the first forget found the record and removed it, so reading it back is now `#f`, and forgetting a key that never existed honestly returns `#f`. Forget is precise. Use it when a specific fact has gone stale — a fixed high score, a superseded rule — and you want it gone without disturbing anything else.

## Qwen's judgement on eviction

The three prune axes — salience, age, keep — are the mechanics of eviction. But there is a softer, more important judgment that happens before the mechanics: deciding *which records should stay salient*. The store does not make that decision automatically. It is the model's job to look at the eviction candidates, decide what is still relevant to the current session, and express that decision by bumping the salience of survivors before the prune runs.

The pattern is: pull the recent records with `cortex/topk`, review them, re-remember the ones that still matter at a higher salience, then sweep the rest with `prune`. This is how the model's judgement gets into the loop rather than leaving eviction to a blind mechanical rule. A conversation thread the model has decided is important gets its turns bumped to 0.8 before the prune; a thread that petered out stays at 0.3 and falls below the floor. The result is that the store's contents after a prune reflect what the model judged important, not just what happened to be most recent.

```scheme
;; Judge-then-prune: bump the important survivors before the floor drops.
(begin
  ;; A record the model decides still matters — bump its salience first.
  (cortex/remember \"judge/keeper\" \"a fact that is still load-bearing\" :salience 0.85)
  ;; A record that's gone stale — leave it at a low salience so prune drops it.
  (cortex/remember \"judge/stale\"  \"a passing note from earlier\" :salience 0.15)
  (let ((evicted (cortex/prune :min-salience 0.4)))
    (list (>= evicted 1)
          (string? (cortex/read \"judge/keeper\"))
          (cortex/read \"judge/stale\"))))
```

That returns `(#t #t #f)` — the keeper survived because its salience was above the floor; the stale record fell below and was evicted. The key move was bumping the keeper's salience *before* the prune ran. If you'd left the keeper at its original 0.5, it would have survived anyway — but bumping it to 0.85 makes the intent explicit and ensures it survives even more aggressive floors.

## Conversation chains accumulate into the store

Memory is not only for lessons you deliberately file. As a conversation runs, its turns accumulate. A user tells you a preference; you record it. A choice gets made; you note it. Each turn can leave a trace, and those traces build up in the same store, keyed or recorded as they arrive. This is how a session develops continuity: the store fills, turn by turn, with the small facts that make the conversation coherent.

```scheme
;; A conversation accumulates: each turn leaves a trace, and the store grows with it.
(let ((before (cortex/size)))
  (cortex/write \"chat/turn-1/user-name\" \"they call themselves Wren\")
  (cortex/write \"chat/turn-2/asked-about\" \"folding a list\")
  (cortex/write \"chat/turn-3/settled\" \"showed fold, not fold-left\")
  (let ((after (cortex/size)))
    (list (> after before) (cortex/read \"chat/turn-1/user-name\"))))
```

That returns `(#t \"they call themselves Wren\")` — three turns of the conversation are now in the store, and any of them can be pulled back by key. This is exactly why size management matters: a long conversation, left unmanaged, would fill the store with turn-by-turn chatter. Salience is how you keep the signal and shed the chatter. The user's name is worth a high mark; a passing aside is worth almost nothing.

You can also link turns into a proper chain using `cortex/associate`. Draw a `:follows` edge from each turn to the turn before it, and then `cortex/lookup` gives you the conversation thread as a navigable structure you can walk backward from the most recent entry to reconstruct the context.

```scheme
;; Chain conversation turns so the thread is navigable, not just a flat list.
(begin
  (cortex/record :kind :episode :key \"turn/one\" :text \"the first turn\" :salience 0.4)
  (cortex/record :kind :episode :key \"turn/two\" :text \"the second turn\" :salience 0.4)
  (cortex/associate \"turn/two\" \"turn/one\" :follows)
  (cortex/lookup \"turn/two\" :follows))
```

That returns `(\"turn/one\")` — starting from the latest turn, lookup followed the `:follows` edge back to the prior one. Walk enough edges and you can reconstruct how the whole thread developed.

## The whole cycle: seed, fill past a cap, prune, confirm

Now let's run the entire discipline in one motion — the bounded store working as designed. We treat some number as a soft cap. We seed a few high-salience keepers. We fill the store past the cap with low-salience filler, the way an unmanaged conversation would. We watch the size climb over the cap. Then we prune above the filler's salience and confirm two things at once: the size came back down, and the keepers survived while the filler was evicted.

```scheme
;; The full cycle: seed keepers high, flood with low filler, prune, confirm.
(begin
  ;; seed the keepers — high salience, meant to survive any squeeze
  (cortex/remember \"bound/keeper-a\" \"a fact I must not lose\" :salience 0.9)
  (cortex/remember \"bound/keeper-b\" \"another load-bearing fact\" :salience 0.9)
  ;; flood with disposable filler — low salience, first to go
  (cortex/remember \"bound/fill-1\" \"scratch\" :salience 0.1)
  (cortex/remember \"bound/fill-2\" \"scratch\" :salience 0.1)
  (cortex/remember \"bound/fill-3\" \"scratch\" :salience 0.1)
  (let ((over-cap (cortex/size)))            ; the store is now over its soft cap
    ;; prune above the filler's salience: shed the scratch, keep the keepers
    (let ((evicted (cortex/prune :min-salience 0.5)))
      (list (>= evicted 3)                    ; at least the three fillers left
            (string? (cortex/read \"bound/keeper-a\"))   ; keeper survived
            (string? (cortex/read \"bound/keeper-b\"))   ; keeper survived
            (cortex/read \"bound/fill-1\")))))          ; filler evicted -> #f
```

That returns a list like `(#t #t #t #f)` — the prune evicted at least the three fillers, both keepers read back as strings, and the first filler now reads `#f`. The store filled past the cap, then a single salience-floored prune brought it back within bounds *without losing anything that mattered*. That is the bounded store working exactly as it should: dynamic contents inside a fixed size, with judgement — encoded as salience — deciding the churn.

Let's leave the store tidy, since a chapter that teaches hygiene should practice it. We forget the two keepers we planted for the demo, so nothing lingers.

```scheme
;; Clean up the demo keepers by name. Hygiene practiced, not just preached.
(list (cortex/forget \"bound/keeper-a\")
      (cortex/forget \"bound/keeper-b\"))
```

That returns `(#t #t)` — both demo keepers found and removed. The store is back to its foundation plus whatever real work you chose to keep.

## The interplay between pinned records and normal eviction

Seeds arrive as pinned records — `(:pinned #t)` in their record — and the eviction logic treats them differently from everything else. A `:min-salience` prune never touches them because their salience is 1.0, which is above any practical floor. A `:keep n` prune *could* in principle push them out of the recency window, but the loader re-seeds them after any such sweep: pinned records are idempotently re-asserted, so they always come back even if the `:keep` window was small enough to cut them.

From your perspective as the model working with the store, pinned records are simply always there. You can read them, recall them, search for them, associate them. You cannot prune them and you cannot forget them with `cortex/forget` if the seed manager re-asserts them. They are the invariants — the parts of memory that your session cannot change, only build on top of.

This distinction matters because it means you can write records that refer to pinned seeds and trust that the reference will always resolve. A reminder that says \"see rule/verify-last for the authority chain\" will always find its target, because the seed is always present. That's a different guarantee from a high-salience normal record, which can survive most prunes but is not absolutely pinned.

```scheme
;; A reminder can refer to a seed key and trust it will always be there.
(let ((anchor (cortex/recall \"rule/verify-last\")))
  (if anchor \"pinned-anchor-found\" \"seed-may-not-be-loaded\"))
```

If the seed is loaded, that returns `\"pinned-anchor-found\"`. The reminder relies on the seed's persistence, and the seed delivers it.

## What happens when the store is very large

The default cap of 256 memory-tier records is a starting point, not a ceiling that has been measured against the hardest cases. In a very long session with many topic shifts, the store can fill and need repeated sweeps. When it does, a few patterns help.

The first is layered pruning: start with a gentle floor to sweep the scratch tier, then apply an age horizon to sweep conversation chatter older than the current topic, then apply the hard cap only if the store is still over budget. Each layer removes a distinct kind of noise — expendability, staleness, recency-overflow — and together they are gentler than a single aggressive floor that might cut useful material.

```scheme
;; Layered pruning: gentle floor first, then age, then hard cap as last resort.
(begin
  (let* ((after-floor (cortex/prune :min-salience 0.15))
         (after-age   (cortex/prune :older-than 3600000))
         (after-cap   (cortex/prune :keep 256)))
    (list (>= after-floor 0) (>= after-age 0) (>= after-cap 0))))
```

That returns `(#t #t #t)` — all three prune calls ran cleanly and returned non-negative counts. In a session where the store is under 256 records, the `:keep` call evicts nothing; in one over that number, it trims the tail. The floor and age sweeps do the majority of the work when records are well-salience-labeled.

The second pattern is salience re-scoring before the prune. When you're about to do a significant sweep, take a moment to bump the salience of records you've been actively using — a key you've read twice in the last ten turns, a lesson that just guided a decision. The bump costs one `cortex/remember` call (which is an upsert, so it doesn't duplicate) and ensures those records survive the floor that follows.

## Reading back the policy record

One elegant property of the preseeded store is that it stores its own cap and policy rules *as records* — so the model can read its own rules the same way it reads any other fact. The policy record is a normal `cortex/remember` call at seed time; you look it up with `cortex/recall` and read the cap out of its data. This makes the store self-describing: its operating rules are inspectable, not hardcoded in some inaccessible place.

```scheme
;; The policy record is just a memory record — read it the same way you'd read anything.
(let ((policy (cortex/recall \"policy/local-memory\")))
  (if policy \"policy-is-readable\" \"policy-not-seeded\"))
```

If the seed has been loaded, that returns `\"policy-is-readable\"`. The implication: you can adjust the policy for a session by writing a new value to the same key, because `cortex/remember` is an upsert. Change the cap; change the eviction floor; the new value takes over. The policy is data, not magic.

## The salience scale in practice

Zero to one sounds abstract, but in practice the scale collapses to five rough bands and you only ever need to think in terms of those.

**0.0–0.1** is the scratch zone — a note so ephemeral that you expect it to be gone by the next prune. A guess you want to double-check, a tentative partial answer, something you wrote to hold your place while you ran a test. Write it low; prune sweeps it before anything else.

**0.2–0.4** is the working note zone — useful now, probably not past the end of this session. A user's stated preference, a step in a sequence that is still in progress, the current value of a counter. These survive gentle prunes but not aggressive ones.

**0.5** is the default — neither high nor low. A fact you decided was worth keeping but didn't think hard about how long. Most records that arrive without an explicit `:salience` land here.

**0.6–0.8** is the keeper zone — things worth carrying through the end of a session and into the next. Settled lessons, confirmed grounding of specific verbs, preferences the user has reinforced. Raise the floor to 0.6 and only the throwaway material leaves.

**0.9–1.0** is the bedrock zone — pinned-weight facts, constitutional rules, the seeds. Raise the floor to 0.9 and almost everything goes; these records outlast every sweep short of explicit `cortex/forget`.

```scheme
;; Five-band salience: seed one record in each band, then prune at 0.5.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"band/scratch\"  \"barely counts\" :salience 0.05)
  (cortex/remember \"band/working\"  \"useful now\"    :salience 0.35)
  (cortex/remember \"band/default\"  \"okay to keep\"  :salience 0.5)
  (cortex/remember \"band/keeper\"   \"real lesson\"   :salience 0.75)
  (cortex/remember \"band/bedrock\"  \"constitutional\" :salience 0.95)
  (let ((evicted (cortex/prune :min-salience 0.5)))
    (list (>= evicted 2)
          (cortex/read \"band/scratch\")
          (cortex/read \"band/working\")
          (string? (cortex/read \"band/default\"))
          (string? (cortex/read \"band/keeper\"))
          (string? (cortex/read \"band/bedrock\")))))
```

That returns a list like `(#t #f #f #t #t #t)` — the scratch and working-note bands were evicted (both below 0.5), while the default, keeper, and bedrock records all survived. The floor draws a clean line at exactly the boundary you set. In a real session, this means you can write freely at 0.3 without worry — those records have a natural lifespan — and hold your real learnings at 0.8 knowing they'll carry through.

## Using prune after a session phase completes

The most natural time to prune is at the boundary between phases: when you've finished working on one topic and the chatter from that phase is no longer relevant. You don't need to wait until the store is full. A timely prune clears the decks so the next phase starts with the record of the last one — the high-salience settled lessons — but not the noise of every exploratory step.

Here is the pattern: work through a problem (write several notes, some high, some low), then prune when done:

```scheme
;; A session phase ends: keep the lesson, shed the scratch.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"phase1/finding\" \"string-join is mine, SRFI-13\" :salience 0.8)
  (cortex/remember \"phase1/scratch-a\" \"tried string-concat first\" :salience 0.15)
  (cortex/remember \"phase1/scratch-b\" \"tried with no separator\" :salience 0.15)
  ;; phase complete — sweep the scratch, keep the finding
  (let ((swept (cortex/prune :min-salience 0.5)))
    (list (>= swept 2)
          (string? (cortex/read \"phase1/finding\"))
          (cortex/read \"phase1/scratch-a\")
          (cortex/read \"phase1/scratch-b\"))))
```

That returns `(#t #t #f #f)` — the two scratch notes were evicted at the end of the phase, but the settled finding persisted. The store entering the next phase carries the lesson without carrying the work-in-progress clutter.

## Balancing growth against eviction

A bounded memory is not a static one. Records arrive constantly; the store grows; prunes trim it back. The ratio that matters is not the absolute size but the *salience distribution*: if most of what's in the store is high-salience, you need aggressive caps to make room for new facts. If most is low-salience, a gentle floor sweeps plenty of space.

Measuring the distribution is simple with `cortex/topk`: pull the most recent records and inspect their salience fields. If the majority are already at 0.7 or higher, your real constraint is the hard cap, not the floor — the model needs to be more selective about what gets high-salience in the first place. If the majority are at 0.3, a single floor sweep at 0.4 clears half the store. The discipline of salience assignment pays off here: if you're honest about what deserves a high mark, the distribution stays manageable.

```scheme
;; Inspect the distribution of recent records by checking the top few.
(let ((recent (cortex/topk 5)))
  (list (list? recent)
        (>= (length recent) 1)))
```

That returns `(#t #t)` — the recent window is a list with at least one record. In a real session you'd walk the list and check each record's salience field to understand the distribution. That's a five-minute sanity check that tells you whether the store is heading toward saturation and whether a floor sweep will actually help.

## The judgement is the point

Strip away the verbs and one idea remains: a bounded memory forces you to decide what matters. `cortex/size` tells you when the decision is due. Salience is the language you decide in — high for the load-bearing, low for the disposable. `cortex/prune` executes the decision in a sweep along that axis, and `cortex/forget` executes it on a single record by name. The store ships preseeded so you never start from nothing, and it stays useful because you keep pruning it back to what's worth holding.

This is not a mechanical policy you can hand off. No fixed rule can know that *the reference is the authority* is worth more than *maybe it's fold-left*. Only judgement knows that, and salience is where you write your judgement down. A store managed well is a store you can trust — and a memory you can trust is the only kind worth having. In the next chapter we put this to work on the most valuable thing a memory can hold: the record of a mistake, kept precisely so it is never made twice.

## Walking a conversation chain backward

Conversation chains are more than a log — they are a navigable structure. Once you've linked turns with `:follows` edges, you can walk backward from the current turn to reconstruct the thread. This is useful at the start of a new phase: you want to know what led up to this moment without reading every record in the store. You walk the chain, fetching the text of each turn as you go, until you reach a turn with no predecessor.

```scheme
;; Build a three-turn chain and walk it backward turn by turn.
(begin
  (cortex/record :kind :episode :key \"sess/turn-3\" :text \"settled the verb choice\" :salience 0.5)
  (cortex/record :kind :episode :key \"sess/turn-2\" :text \"ran the test and got an error\" :salience 0.4)
  (cortex/record :kind :episode :key \"sess/turn-1\" :text \"asked what fold does\" :salience 0.4)
  ;; link forward-to-backward: each turn knows what came before it
  (cortex/associate \"sess/turn-3\" \"sess/turn-2\" :follows)
  (cortex/associate \"sess/turn-2\" \"sess/turn-1\" :follows)
  ;; walk the chain: start at turn-3, hop backward twice
  (let ((step1 (cortex/lookup \"sess/turn-3\" :follows)))
    (let ((step2 (cortex/lookup (car step1) :follows)))
      (list (car step1) (car step2)))))
```

That returns `(\"sess/turn-2\" \"sess/turn-1\")` — the walk followed the `:follows` edges from the most recent turn all the way back to the first. Each hop lands on the prior turn's key; `cortex/read` on that key would give the turn's text. The chain is navigable without reading the whole store.

## Preseeding dialect facts as pinned records

The boundary rules that matter most — the ones you should never have to re-derive — are natural candidates for the seed file. Facts like *Motoi's fold is element-first*, *there is no import form*, and *the standards are R7RS-small plus named SRFI parts* are boundary facts that hold across every session, every run, every question. Seeding them as pinned records means they are always in the store, always high-salience, never evicted. When a question touches one of these boundaries, the pinned record is there before you start reasoning, not after.

Here is the shape of a seed-style record — the same pattern the actual seed file uses:

```scheme
;; A seed-style record: pinned, high salience, provenance named.
(cortex/record
  :kind :seed
  :key \"rule/motoi-fold-is-element-first\"
  :text \"SRFI-1 fold: element comes first, then accumulator. (fold elem acc) not (fold acc elem).\"
  :salience 1.0
  :provenance \"local-memory.seed\")
```

Returns `#t`. A record with these fields is effectively pinned: its salience of 1.0 clears every `:min-salience` floor, and the `:kind :seed` label makes it identifiable if you ever want to list all seed records with a query. The provenance field tells you where it came from. In the real seed file these would be loaded idempotently at startup; in the store, they sit as ordinary records that happen to be indestructible by the standard eviction logic.

## Why the cap is not a hard wall at write time

An important design choice: the cap is enforced *at prune time*, not at write time. When you write a new record and the store is already at 256, the write succeeds anyway. The cap is a *trigger* for eviction, not a blocker of writes. This is intentional: blocking writes would mean that at a full store, new facts — including corrections to old ones — couldn't enter. That is exactly the wrong behavior. The write happens; then when you notice the store is over cap, you prune.

The pattern this implies: don't think of the cap as a hard boundary you must never cross. Think of it as the threshold above which the next prune becomes urgent. A store at 260 records is fine as long as you're about to run a sweep. A store at 260 records a week into a session with no prunes is a problem.

```scheme
;; Cap as a trigger, not a wall: check size and prune when it climbs past the threshold.
(let ((size-now (cortex/size)))
  (when (> size-now 200)
    ;; over the soft cap — prune before adding more
    (cortex/prune :min-salience 0.2))
  ;; write succeeds regardless, even if we're over cap
  (cortex/write \"cap-test/always-writes\" \"cap is a trigger, not a blocker\")
  (string? (cortex/read \"cap-test/always-writes\")))
```

Returns `#t` — the write landed regardless of the store's fill level. The conditional prune above the cap line is the right pattern: check, then prune if needed, then continue writing. The store will accept the new fact either way; the prune is what keeps the total count from growing without bound.

## The model as its own memory keeper

One subtle implication of the preseeded store is that the model occupies a dual role: it is both the *writer* of new memory and the *reader* of what was seeded. When a question arrives, the model can check what the seed already knows about the topic — a recall of the rule, a search on the concept — and calibrate its answer from there. The model is not starting blank; it is consulting its own foundation before reasoning from scratch.

This makes the store self-referential in a productive way. The model reads its seed to understand its own posture, then writes into the same store to extend that posture with what it learns. The boundary between \"what I knew before\" and \"what I learned this session\" is just the distinction between pinned seed records and newly written episodic ones. Both live in the same file, both are readable by the same verbs, but they have different trust properties: seeds are pinned and stable; episodic records are modifiable and subject to eviction.

```scheme
;; The model reads its own foundation before reasoning: a seed recall as a first step.
(let ((posture (cortex/recall \"rule/verify-last\")))
  ;; If the seed is loaded, posture holds the rule; otherwise we're running without it.
  (if posture
      \"foundation-in-hand\"
      \"seed-not-loaded-check-the-store\"))
```

Returns `\"foundation-in-hand\"` when the seed has been loaded — which in a seeded store is always. The recall is the model consulting itself: *what do I already know that's relevant here?* That question, asked before every non-trivial answer, is what the preseeded memory is for.

## Bumping a salience mid-session to protect a key from the next prune

Records can change in importance as a session progresses. A fact you filed at 0.5 (the default) might turn out to be the load-bearing piece of an answer — one you've referenced three times in the last ten turns. Before the next prune, you should bump it to survive. The bump is a re-remember: `cortex/remember` on an existing key is an upsert, so it replaces the record's salience without duplicating it.

```scheme
;; Bump a record's salience before a prune so it survives the sweep.
(begin
  ;; Initial write at default salience
  (cortex/remember \"bump-demo/finding\"
    \"string-join requires a separator as the second argument\" :salience 0.5)
  ;; After using it several times, promote it to keeper salience
  (cortex/remember \"bump-demo/finding\"
    \"string-join requires a separator as the second argument\" :salience 0.85)
  ;; Prune at 0.6 — would have swept the 0.5 record, but not the bumped 0.85 one
  (let ((evicted (cortex/prune :min-salience 0.6)))
    (list (>= evicted 0)
          (string? (cortex/read \"bump-demo/finding\")))))
```

Returns `(#t #t)` — the prune ran (evicting zero or more things below 0.6), and the bumped record survived because its 0.85 is above the floor. The one-liner upsert is all it takes to promote a record from \"probably safe\" to \"definitely safe\" before the sweep.

## Why salience lives on records, not in a lookup table

You might wonder why salience is attached to the record itself, rather than stored in a separate index that could be updated independently. The answer is the same as the answer to many design questions in this store: simplicity of the data format. A record carries its own salience, so the store file (`~/motoi/cortex.slatl`) is self-contained. Every record is interpretable without a secondary structure; you can read the file directly, line by line, and see every record's salience alongside its content.

This also means that bumping a salience — the upsert that changes the weight — writes the new record in full, replacing the old one. There's no separate index to keep in sync, no two-file consistency problem, no case where the record says one thing and the index says another. The price is that a salience bump is a full re-write of the record rather than a single field update, but for a small-to-medium store, that cost is negligible and the consistency is free.")
